<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
        xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
        xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
            http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-3.6.xsd">

    <!--
      Where ingest_item.acquisition_date came from.

      The column held two different kinds of thing under one name. For a DICOM
      file it was read out of the header — a property of the scan. For an .e2e
      or an image it was whatever the upload workbench had in its date box, and
      that box is the *visit search key*: the operator types a day, the panel
      lists that day's visits, they pick one, and the day they searched with was
      written back onto the file as the day it was acquired. The stored date
      then agreed with the visit by construction, which reads exactly like
      corroboration and is not.

      Observed: ingest_item 5 (OCTMP224.E2E, bound to HAE-002's 2026-09-09
      baseline) carried acquisition_date=2026-09-09 while nothing had read a
      date from the file at all. The file does carry one — 2026-09-09, as it
      happens — but the match was luck, not verification.

      So the date now travels with its provenance:
        file      — read out of the file itself (DICOM header, .e2e exam chunk)
        operator  — asserted by the person uploading; NOT verified
        unknown   — recorded before this column existed (backfill below)
      NULL means no date is known, which is an honest answer and the one the
      reconciliation inbox is expected to show for a file nothing could date.

      Only 'file' is evidence. The bind-time visit-date check keys off it and
      stays quiet for everything else, because warning about a date the
      operator just typed would fire on every single upload.

      Additive; the existing column keeps its values and nothing reads the new
      one as required.
    -->
    <changeSet id="2026-12-03-ingest-acquisition-date-source" author="muw-ophthalmology">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <not>
                <columnExists tableName="ingest_item" columnName="acquisition_date_source"/>
            </not>
        </preConditions>
        <comment>ingest_item.acquisition_date_source — whether the date was read from the file or asserted by an operator.</comment>

        <addColumn tableName="ingest_item">
            <column name="acquisition_date_source" type="VARCHAR(16)"/>
        </addColumn>

        <rollback>
            <dropColumn tableName="ingest_item" columnName="acquisition_date_source"/>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <!--
      Backfill. Every row that predates the column has a date of unknown
      provenance, and there is no way to recover which kind it was: the DICOM
      path and the workbench path wrote the same column the same way. Marking
      them 'unknown' rather than guessing keeps them out of the bind check and
      out of any UI that claims a date is verified.

      Rows with no date stay NULL on both columns.
    -->
    <changeSet id="2026-12-03-ingest-acquisition-date-source-backfill" author="muw-ophthalmology">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <columnExists tableName="ingest_item" columnName="acquisition_date_source"/>
        </preConditions>
        <comment>Existing dates predate the provenance column; mark them unknown rather than guessing.</comment>

        <update tableName="ingest_item">
            <column name="acquisition_date_source" value="unknown"/>
            <where>acquisition_date IS NOT NULL AND acquisition_date_source IS NULL</where>
        </update>

        <rollback>
            <update tableName="ingest_item">
                <column name="acquisition_date_source" value="null"/>
                <where>acquisition_date_source = 'unknown'</where>
            </update>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
